  "answer_text": "U.S. inflation eased slightly overall in 2025, but it didn’t fall steadily. The Consumer Price Index (CPI) rose 2.7% from December 2024 to December 2025, compared with 2.9% over the previous 12 months. Prices were still rising, just a bit more slowly. (bls.gov)\nYear-over-year inflation started at 3.0% in January, fell to 2.3% in April, climbed back to 3.0% in September, and ended the year at 2.7%. The Bureau of Labor Statistics did not publish an October CPI reading because of the federal government shutdown. (bls.gov)\nCore CPI, which excludes food and energy, rose 2.6% over the year. Food prices rose 3.1%, and shelter prices rose 3.2%. (bls.gov)",
